{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The host REBOOTED at ~23:52 UTC, and it changes three things — measured, read-only\n\nGerrit went unreachable (`curl` refused on :443, `git ls-remote` reset). SSM showed `up 0 min`.\nIt is back now. Three consequences, all verified rather than inferred:\n\n### 1. The remediation is STILL required, and the reboot did not help\n\n```\nlsblk   nvme1n1  50G xfs  /var/gerrit  vol06fa2e77a9dd97527\n        nvme2n1  50G  --      --       vol06780b8557d1416b7   <- still raw, still unmounted\n/etc/fstab                -> still no gate-scratch line\n.gate-scratch-required    -> still absent\n.gate-scratch-mounted     -> still absent\nmountpoint gate-scratch   -> is not a mountpoint\n```\n\nThis is the expected behaviour and it is worth stating plainly, because \"have you tried\nrebooting it\" is the obvious wrong idea here: **cloud-init runs user-data ONCE PER INSTANCE, not\nonce per boot.** A reboot does not re-run `user_data.sh`, so it cannot format or mount a volume\nattached after first boot, and it did not. Only an instance REPLACEMENT would — at the cost of\nreplacing the production Gerrit host. This reboot is therefore a direct empirical confirmation of\nthe mechanism this ticket describes, rather than a coincidence.\n\n### 2. Root fell from 81% to 76% — relief, not resolution\n\n```\nbefore reboot   49G of 60G (81%), 12G free\nafter  reboot   46G of 60G (76%), 15G free\n```\n\n~3G of transient files went with the restart (the per-review `reviewbot-*` clones are gone;\n`rebar-gate-snapshots`, `semgrep-mcp` and the loose `tmp*` files survived). The trend that took\nroot from 57% to 81% in eleven hours is unchanged — this bought hours, not a fix.\n\n### 3. DEVICE NAMES ARE NOT A SAFE IDENTIFIER, and I now have a direct observation\n\nAt `up 0 min`, `df -h` reported:\n\n```\n/dev/nvme2n1     50G   11G   40G  21% /var/gerrit\n```\n\nOne minute later `lsblk` reported `nvme1n1 ... /var/gerrit` and `nvme2n1` as the unmounted\ngate-scratch volume — the original mapping. So the two reads DISAGREED about which device node\nbacks `/var/gerrit`, a minute apart, on the same host. Most likely the first was taken while NVMe\nenumeration was still settling.\n\n**Why this matters more than a curiosity.** `/var/gerrit` is Gerrit's data volume, and it is\nmounted by UUID, so it followed its filesystem correctly and nothing was harmed. But the\nremediation for THIS ticket runs `mkfs.xfs` on the gate-scratch device. An operator who had\ncopied `/dev/nvme2n1` out of an earlier note and run `mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme2n1` during that window\nwould have **formatted Gerrit's data volume.**\n\n`infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md` already refuses that, in two independent ways, and I am\nrecording this because the reboot turned a defensive precaution into a demonstrated one:\n\n- §0 and §4 both re-confirm the target by **SERIAL** (`vol06780b8557d1416b7`), never by device\n  name, and §5 writes fstab by **UUID** for the same reason.\n- §4 will not format at all unless `blkid` reports NO filesystem — so even a mis-identified\n  device that already carries xfs is refused rather than overwritten.\n\nNo action is required from this comment. Nothing was mutated; every command above was read-only."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788652480298974001,"uuid":"13623fd1-0901-46f1-8a11-98cffc2e16a9"}